1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:00,650
Part five.

2
00:00:01,075 --> 00:00:01,850
The death watch.

3
00:00:02,262 --> 00:00:05,688
Zitron's sharpest prediction is about the so-called neoclouds.

4
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:08,062
These are companies that raise debt to buy

5
00:00:08,075 --> 00:00:11,450
Nvidia chips, using those same chips as collateral,

6
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:13,862
then rent the computing power to a small

7
00:00:13,875 --> 00:00:15,350
group of AI customers.

8
00:00:15,762 --> 00:00:18,862
Often the same customers Nvidia itself has invested

9
00:00:18,875 --> 00:00:19,062
in.

10
00:00:19,488 --> 00:00:20,562
He has put two of them on what

11
00:00:20,575 --> 00:00:21,763
he calls a death watch.

12
00:00:22,188 --> 00:00:23,512
The first is Nscale,

13
00:00:23,788 --> 00:00:24,900
which, he says,

14
00:00:25,125 --> 00:00:27,675
recently told investors it needs to raise more

15
00:00:27,688 --> 00:00:28,900
money than expected,

16
00:00:29,288 --> 00:00:31,325
and which is still negotiating backing for a

17
00:00:31,338 --> 00:00:34,512
forty-five billion dollar data center project for Anthropic

18
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:35,462
in West Virginia.

19
00:00:35,888 --> 00:00:37,262
The second is Firmus,

20
00:00:37,462 --> 00:00:39,588
an Australian data center company that cut its

21
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:41,262
IPO and then pulled it.

22
00:00:41,675 --> 00:00:43,637
And Lambda, another chip renter,

23
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:45,775
is hoping to go public next year,

24
00:00:46,075 --> 00:00:48,412
which only works if investors still trust the

25
00:00:48,425 --> 00:00:48,925
sector.

26
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:50,862
His point is about confidence.

27
00:00:51,288 --> 00:00:52,638
Banks lent to these companies,

28
00:00:52,788 --> 00:00:55,775
he argues, because they believed Nvidia would step

29
00:00:55,788 --> 00:00:57,375
in if anything went wrong.

30
00:00:57,800 --> 00:00:59,412
Without that implicit guarantee,

31
00:00:59,688 --> 00:01:01,475
a bank asks a simple question:

32
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:03,262
is Nvidia backing this?

33
00:01:03,675 --> 00:01:05,287
And if the answer is we're still waiting

34
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:07,038
to hear, the money doesn't come.

35
00:01:07,450 --> 00:01:09,150
It doesn't need all of them to fail,

36
00:01:09,512 --> 00:01:10,100
he says.

37
00:01:10,525 --> 00:01:12,875
One insolvency could make lenders ask whether the

38
00:01:12,888 --> 00:01:15,012
whole sector is just debt stacked on the

39
00:01:15,025 --> 00:01:17,162
same chips and the same customers.

40
00:01:17,575 --> 00:01:20,100
His guest, Steve from the hardware channel Gamers

41
00:01:20,112 --> 00:01:22,262
Nexus, adds a fair counterpoint.

42
00:01:22,688 --> 00:01:25,300
Blaming everything on one man isn't quite right,

43
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:26,288
he says.

44
00:01:26,712 --> 00:01:28,125
They were all part of this together.

45
00:01:28,538 --> 00:01:29,888
So what does any of this mean for

46
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:31,575
people who never bought a single share?
